analysis:
Both supplied units are BASE-side text of CHANGELOG.md (lines 800-1253). They are historical release notes for v0.9.12 through v0.9.18. The PR only changes documentation: the patch replaces the v0.10.0 candidate header with a v0.10.1 candidate header and adds upgrade and rollout notes. I saw no executable code, configuration, workflow or threat-model changes.

The base text mentions security-relevant features (SSD prefix-cache TTL and write-budget, App Attest and MDM gating, account erasure, log redaction). These are existing descriptions and are not altered by the supplied hunks. I saw no head-side text in these units, so I could not verify any claim in the new v0.10.1 notes. Those notes touch the following, and the next pass should check them against the actual code:
- a coordinator dependency on darkbloom-platform#411 for logprobs refusal;
- the libs/mlx-swift-lm pin bump from 7bed8216 to 6148b6da;
- page-cache memory accounting that may count more memory as available for load admission and KV budgets (relevant to TB-007 and T-041 availability);
- 0.10.0 GLM providers returning a 500 that cools every provider of the model, which could be an availability concern if a consumer sends logprobs to an un-updated coordinator.

The patch excerpt is truncated, so I could not review the full list of head changes. Nothing in the supplied evidence shows a concrete security regression, and the changelog text itself does not change any control from the threat model, including T-041 and SEC-035. I am setting needs_deeper_review=true only because the new head-side notes and the code changes they describe were not visible to me.
